A mechanism that makes it possible to establish a connection between Bitcoin's main system and a sidechain (or a drivechain), that is, a side chain. The two-way peg ensures a fixed value correlation between the bitcoins on the main blockchain and the corresponding assets on the sidechain, thus making it possible to move bitcoins between the two chains. To do this, the bitcoins are temporarily locked on the main blockchain and an equivalent amount of assets is issued on the sidechain. This makes it possible to benefit from the specific advantages of the sidechain, such as faster transactions or improved privacy features, while maintaining the value of the bitcoins used. When users wish to return to the Bitcoin blockchain, the process is reversed: the assets on the sidechain are destroyed and the corresponding bitcoins are unlocked. There are many different two-way peg mechanisms, which can rely on:
* A single trusted third party;
* A federation of entities;
* The miners of the main chain (drivechain).

A company specializing in Lightning payment solutions. Alby offers an ecosystem of complementary products:
* Alby Hub, a *self-custodial* Lightning node that can be self-hosted, deployed in the cloud, or used as an interface for a conventional node;
* the Alby browser extension, which makes it possible to make Lightning payments and to authenticate on websites directly from the browser;
* Alby Go, a mobile application to manage one's payments on the go.

Alby also provides APIs and the *Nostr Wallet Connect* (NWC) protocol to allow developers to integrate Lightning payments into third-party applications.

In the RGB protocol, an *Anchor* represents a set of client-side data used to prove the inclusion of a unique commitment in a transaction. In the RGB protocol, an *Anchor* is made up of the following elements:
* The Bitcoin transaction identifier (TXID) of the *witness transaction*;
* The *Multi Protocol Commitment* (MPC) proof, comprising the depth, the cofactor, and the Merkle proof;
* The *Extra Transaction Proof* (ETP), which provides the additional data needed to validate a *Tapret*-type commitment (not required for *Opret*).

An *Anchor* therefore serves to establish a verifiable link between a specific Bitcoin transaction and private data validated by the RGB protocol. It guarantees that this data is indeed included in the blockchain, without its exact content being publicly exposed.

Information used to receive bitcoins. An address is generally built by hashing a public key, using `SHA256` and `RIPEMD160`, and adding metadata to this digest. The public keys used to build a receiving address are part of the user's wallet and are therefore derived from its seed. For example, SegWit addresses are composed of the following information: 
* An HRP to designate “bitcoin”: `bc`; 
* A separator: `1`; 
* The SegWit version used: `q` or `p`; 
* The payload: the digest of the public key (or directly the public key in the case of Taproot); 
* The checksum: a BCH code.

But a receiving address can also represent something else depending on the script template used. For example, P2SH addresses are built using the hash of the script. Taproot addresses, for their part, directly contain the tweaked public key (*tweaked*) without it being hashed.

A receiving address can be represented as a string of alphanumeric characters or as a QR code. Each address can be used several times, but this is a highly discouraged practice. Indeed, in order to maintain a certain level of privacy, it is advised to use each Bitcoin address only once. A new one must be generated for every incoming payment to one's wallet. An address is encoded in `Bech32` for SegWit V0 addresses, in `Bech32m` for SegWit V1 addresses, and in `Base58check` for Legacy addresses. From a technical point of view, receiving bitcoin translates into possessing the private key associated with a public key (and therefore with an address). When someone receives bitcoins, the sender updates the existing constraint on their spending so that only the recipient can now have this power.

![BIP-84 derivation tree linking the master key to the addresses, via the purpose, coin type, account and chain levels.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LoicPandul/Dictionnaire-de-Bitcoin/bab568e32351cc9f1240f49f5feedc242123d101/definitions/fr/a/adresse-de-reception/assets/image-1.png)

A practice that brings together all the methods for tracing bitcoin flows on the blockchain. In general, chain analysis relies on observing characteristics in samples of earlier transactions. It then consists of spotting these same characteristics in a transaction one wishes to analyze, and drawing plausible interpretations from them. This problem-solving method, based on a practical approach to find a good enough solution, is what is called a heuristic. To put it simply, chain analysis is done in two main steps: 
* Spotting known characteristics;
* Deducing hypotheses.

One of the goals of chain analysis is to group various activities on Bitcoin in order to determine whether they were carried out by a single user. Afterwards, it will be possible to try to link this bundle of activities to a real-world identity through an entry point. It is important to understand that chain analysis is not an exact science. It relies on heuristics derived from earlier observations or logical interpretations. These rules make it possible to obtain fairly reliable results, but never absolute precision. In other words, chain analysis always involves an element of plausibility in the conclusions reached. For example, one can estimate with more or less certainty that two addresses belong to the same entity, but total certainty will always be out of reach. The whole objective of chain analysis lies precisely in aggregating various heuristics in order to minimize the risk of error. It is, in a way, an accumulation of evidence that lets us come closer to reality. These famous heuristics can be grouped into different categories: 
* Transaction *patterns* (or transaction models);
* Heuristics internal to the transaction;
* Heuristics external to the transaction. 

Note that the first two heuristics on Bitcoin were formulated by Satoshi Nakamoto himself. He sets them out in section 10 of the *White Paper*. It is interesting to observe that these two heuristics still hold a preeminent place in chain analysis today. They are the CIOH (*Common Input Ownership Heuristic*) and address reuse.
